; =====================================================================
; serial-control-operator.cddl
;
; VERBATIM.  Both fenced regions below are draft-ietf-cbor-serialization-08's
; own CDDL, reproduced byte-for-byte with their three-space page margin,
; from Section 8 ("CDDL Serialization Control Operator") - lines 967-969
; and 974-975 of the cached text rendering
; (.cache/draft-ietf-cbor-serialization.txt, sha256
; a612e6c4f674da61b1c888431a06dca13457aaea53c1cd86c8b50f8999b47112).
; Two fences, not two files: they are the two worked examples of a single
; artifact, the `.serial` control operator, and neither stands alone.
; Nothing here is ours.
; =====================================================================
;
; WHAT SECTION 8 ADDS.  `.serial` is the one piece of new *syntax* this
; draft contributes, and Section 10 asks IANA to register it in the
; "CDDL Control Operators" registry.  Its whole definition is three
; sentences of prose, which no fence below contains, so they are quoted
; here rather than restated:
;
;     "The ".serial" control operator specifies the serialization of any
;     type in CDDL, including the serialization of the whole document or
;     byte-string-wrapped sub-parts."
;
;     "The controller (the right-hand side) MUST be either "prefp" or
;     "dtrm", specifying preferred-plus (Section 4) or deterministic
;     (Section 5) serialization, respectively."
;
;     "The scope of .serial applies recursively through nested arrays and
;     maps, but does not extend into byte strings or other data items
;     that happen to contain encoded CBOR.  Every instance of embedded
;     CBOR that requires specific serialization must specify it
;     explicitly."
;
; So the grammar of the operator is `<type> .serial (prefp / dtrm)`, the
; controller is one of exactly two literal names, and new controller
; values "are possible but are highly discouraged.  Standards action is
; required to add them."
;
; WHAT IT DOES NOT CONSTRAIN.  Everything the two serializations are.
; `.serial` is a *hook*: it names a rule set for a validator to apply,
; in the same way RFC 8610's `.cbor` names a nested grammar.  A CDDL
; tool that does not implement this draft will parse `stuff .serial
; dtrm` and check `stuff` alone, which is why no file in this directory
; can be fed to a validator and be said to have checked determinism.
; The shortest-head predicate, the float-width choice, the single
; permitted NaN and the map key ordering all live behind this operator,
; not in it - see prefp-head.derived.abnf and the two derived pseudocode
; files for the parts this library renders machine-checkable.
;
; THE SECOND FENCE IS THE DOCUMENT-WIDE IDIOM.  Section 8 introduces it
; as "the first lines of a CDDL document as follows specify that
; "my-protocol" be serialized with preferred-plus" - that is, you apply
; `.serial` once, to the root rule, and it reaches every nested array
; and map beneath it.  `my-protocol = ...` is the draft's ellipsis for
; "the rest of your protocol", not CDDL that parses.
;
; THE FIRST FENCE IS THE WRAPPING IDIOM, and it is the one that matters
; for a signing envelope.  `#6.24(bytes .cbor deterministic-stuff)` is
; tag 24 (encoded CBOR data item) whose byte-string content is itself
; CBOR matching `deterministic-stuff`.  Because the scope sentence above
; stops `.serial` at a byte string boundary, the determinism here is
; carried by the inner `.serial dtrm`, which had to be written
; explicitly; an outer `.serial` on the wrapping protocol would not have
; reached inside.  Appendix K.2 makes the same point in prose for COSE:
; "determinism requirements do not extend into CBOR inside of byte
; strings.  Once CBOR data is wrapped in a byte string, its internal
; encoding is treated as opaque and is not subject to surrounding
; serialization constraints."
;
; NOTE ON `bytes`.  The first fence writes `bytes`, the prelude synonym
; for `bstr`.  That is the source's spelling and is kept.
; =====================================================================

; Introduced in Section 8 by: "For example, the following specifies that
; a message or protocol described by "stuff" is deterministically
; serialized and wrapped in a byte string:"
; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM (draft-ietf-cbor-serialization-08, Section 8) ----
   stuff = ...
   deterministic-stuff = stuff .serial dtrm
   wrapped-deterministic-stuff = #6.24(bytes .cbor deterministic-stuff)
; ---- END VERBATIM ----

; Introduced in Section 8 by: "For another example, the first lines of a
; CDDL document as follows specify that "my-protocol" be serialized with
; preferred-plus."
; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM (draft-ietf-cbor-serialization-08, Section 8) ----
   my-prefp-protocol = my-protocol .serial prefp
   my-protocol = ...
; ---- END VERBATIM ----
